# The two signature spellings of a nonprimitive formal, `(Model: v)` and `(@: Model v)`, are ONE reference transport
# (the book, "A signature is not executed": for nonprimitive A, formals (A: b) and (@: A b) describe the same reference
# transport with candidate admission to A; next_core_tasks_v2.md K03; steps/generated-diagnostic-migration-20260930.md
# D4).  In the body v is therefore a reference binding whichever way the header spelled it, and `v: w` is the same
# operation for both: v's rebinding to w after w's admission to Model -- not a call of v (a reference binding does not
# auto-call its referent), and never a copy.  Reading through v afterwards reads w's cell; the caller's Structure is
# untouched.  Success is 7.
#
# Formerly unit_value_formal_call_refused, which asserted the opposite: that `(Model: v)` made v a callable VALUE and
# `v: w` its call, refused for the argument -- the obsolete distinction between the spellings.  Its refusal is now
# unit_formal_spelling_rebind_other_refused: the SAME rebinding, refused by the admission, not by an arity check.
Model:
    size_t: value 1U
end: Model

fn: byColon (Model: v) size_t
    Model: w
    w\value: 41U
    v: w
    return: v\value
end: byColon

fn: byAt (@: Model v) size_t
    Model: w
    w\value: 43U
    v: w
    return: v\value
end: byAt

fn: check () int
    Model: mo
    size_t: a
    size_t: b
    mo\value: 9U
    a: byColon(mo)
    if: a != 41U
        return: 1
    b: byAt(@mo)
    if: b != 43U
        return: 2
    if: mo\value != 9U
        return: 3
return: 7

sendMessage: exit(exit_code: check(); stdout: ""; stderr: "")
return
